##Quick Setup:-

Clone
git clone https://github.com/Mehakgujarati02/smartdialer.git
cd smartdialer

Install dependencies:-
pip install -r requirements.txt

Run the simulator:-
streamlit run app.py

Run tests:-
python -m pytest -q
No external database or telecom service is required.

Design Decisions

The prototype intentionally uses:

Python for simple, fast prototyping
Dataclasses and in-memory state for the core application
JSON + file locking for cross-process persistence
Streamlit for the interactive simulator
Mock telecom providers instead of external services

The storage layer is isolated so that a production database can be introduced later without changing the core dialing logic.

Known Limitations

This is a prototype, so a few limitations remain:

Safety Controller call counts are maintained in memory.
Answer-rate collapse does not automatically trigger fallback yet.
An agent going offline during an active call is detected but not automatically reassigned.
The live UI uses the simpler single-process implementation.
Benchmarking currently focuses on core operations rather than full load testing.
Future Improvements
Replace file-based persistence with a production database
Add a proper priority queue for borrower selection
Improve automatic recovery and reassignment
Add distributed worker support
Add dedicated load-testing infrastructure
Improve predictive pacing with more advanced forecasting


"How would you build a SmartDialer that gets as much of the utilization benefit of predictive dialing as possible, while retaining the deterministic safety characteristics of progressive dialing?"


                    Campaign
                       |
                       v
                Predictive Pacing
                       |
              "I suggest N calls"
                       |
                       v
              Safety Controller
              /      |       \
           Approve  Reduce   Reject
                       |
                       v
                 Call Allocator
                       |
                       v
               Telecom Provider

The Predictive Pacing Engine does not place calls directly.

It continuously looks at things such as:
Current available agents
Recent answer/connect rate
Average talk time
Wrap-up time
Calls currently ringing
Current abandonment rate
Provider health

and calculates how aggressively it can dial.

For example:
10 agents are available and the recent answer rate is 25%, so the predictive engine may suggest dialing more than 10 numbers.

But that suggestion then goes through the Safety Controller.

The Safety Controller enforces hard limits such as:
Maximum concurrent calls
Maximum pacing ratio
Maximum acceptable abandonment rate
Provider health
Available agent capacity

So even if the predictive engine says "dial 30 calls", the Safety Controller might say:
"Only 15 are safe right now."

This is very close to how modern predictive dialers are designed: pacing adapts to live answer rates and agent availability, while abandonment controls act as a hard ceiling rather than something the pacing engine can simply ignore.

The important part: feedback loop
Your predictive engine shouldn't use a fixed ratio forever.
It should continuously learn from what actually happened:

Dial calls
    ↓
Observe answers
    ↓
Measure abandonment
    ↓
Update answer-rate estimate
    ↓
Recalculate pacing
    ↓
Safety Controller
    ↓
Dial again

So if the answer rate suddenly goes from 25% → 50%, the system automatically becomes more conservative because many more people are answering.
If the answer rate drops, it can safely increase pacing to keep agents productive,
That adaptive feedback loop is the real benefit of predictive dialing.
